    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d297">

                <pb facs="d297-01" n="1" xml:id="d297-01"/>

                <head>297. Interagency Intelligence Memorandum 76–023<anchor n="1" xml:id="d297-f1"/>
                    <anchor n="2" xml:id="d297-f2"/>
                </head>
                <dateline>
                    <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1976-05-25"
                        >May 25, 1976</date>
                </dateline>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>SUBJECT:</label>
                    <item>Interagency Intelligence Memorandum: The Military Threat to Zaire</item>
                </list>
                <p>The attached interagency intelligence memorandum concludes that Zaire does not
                    face external military attack during the next year or so. It points out that the
                    major problem facing President Mobutu is an internal one stemming from the
                    country’s parlous economic situation. This could lead to subversive or insurgent
                    activities in Zaire as well as to external meddling in the situation.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        George Bush
                    </signed>
                </closer>


                <xi:include href="d297-a1.xml" xpointer="d297-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d297-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d297-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Central Intelligence Agency, NIC Files, Job 91R00884R, Box 9, Folder 14,
                    IIM: Military Threat to Zaire. Secret; Noforn; Nocontract; Orcon. Transmitted by covering memorandum from DCI
                        Bush to Kissinger on May 25. (Ibid.)</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d297-f2" n="2" xml:id="d297-f2-content">
                <p>The interagency study examined the military threat to Zaire and concluded that it
                    did not face external military attack during the next year or so.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
